From 223860241c57113e177b622216baa805b3501eb9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adam <adam@example.invalid>
Date: Wed, 3 Jun 2026 14:10:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] alarmtimer: preserve sub-second offset when arming the
 suspend RTC alarm

alarmtimer_suspend() programs the wakeup alarm from the current RTC time:

	rtc_read_time(rtc, &tm);
	now = rtc_tm_to_ktime(tm);
	...
	now = ktime_add(now, min);
	rtc_timer_start(rtc, &rtctimer, now, 0);

rtc_read_time() has one-second resolution, so rtc_tm_to_ktime() floors the
current time and discards its sub-second fraction.  rtc_timer_start() later
converts the alarm back with rtc_ktime_to_tm(), which rounds any non-zero
nanosecond part up to the next whole second.  That round-up compensates for
the fractional part of @min, but not for the fraction that was already
dropped from @now.  The programmed alarm is therefore

	alarm = ceil(floor(now) + min)

which is up to one second earlier than the requested expiry whenever

	frac(now) + frac(min) > 1

On an s2idle resume this makes the RTC fire slightly before the in-kernel
deadline.  A CLOCK_BOOTTIME_ALARM timerfd armed for the same deadline is
then not yet readable at resume time, so a consumer that classifies the
wake source with a zero-timeout poll() on that timerfd -- as
systemd-sleep's suspend-then-hibernate path does -- treats the wake as
spurious and skips the hibernation step.  The observable result is an
intermittent, sub-second-phase-dependent "suspend-then-hibernate wakes and
stays up" failure.

Recover the discarded fraction from the system clock before adding @min, so
the alarm is derived from the true current time.  Only the sub-second part
of CLOCK_REALTIME is used, so the result is unaffected by any absolute skew
between the system clock and the RTC; the existing rtc_ktime_to_tm()
round-up then guarantees the alarm is never programmed earlier than the
requested expiry.

Signed-off-by: Your Name <you@example.com>
---
 kernel/time/alarmtimer.c | 19 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+)

diff --git a/kernel/time/alarmtimer.c b/kernel/time/alarmtimer.c
index b64db40..ac62a50 100644
--- a/kernel/time/alarmtimer.c
+++ b/kernel/time/alarmtimer.c
@@ -13,6 +13,7 @@
  * Author: John Stultz <john.stultz@linaro.org>
  */
 #include <linux/time.h>
+#include <linux/math64.h>
 #include <linux/hrtimer.h>
 #include <linux/timerqueue.h>
 #include <linux/rtc.h>
@@ -266,6 +267,24 @@ static int alarmtimer_suspend(struct device *dev)
 	rtc_read_time(rtc, &tm);
 	now = rtc_tm_to_ktime(tm);
 
+	/*
+	 * rtc_read_time() has one-second resolution, so rtc_tm_to_ktime()
+	 * floored @now and dropped its sub-second part. The rtc_ktime_to_tm()
+	 * in rtc_timer_start() below rounds the programmed alarm up by a second,
+	 * which compensates for frac(@min) but not for the fraction discarded
+	 * here -- so without this the alarm can be programmed up to one second
+	 * early, and on an s2idle resume the wakeup precedes the in-kernel
+	 * deadline that a CLOCK_BOOTTIME_ALARM timerfd (e.g. systemd-sleep's
+	 * suspend-then-hibernate poll) is waiting on. Add back the sub-second
+	 * part of the system clock -- only the fraction, so any absolute skew
+	 * between the system clock and the RTC is irrelevant.
+	 */
+	{
+		u64 now_ns = ktime_get_real_ns();
+
+		now = ktime_add_ns(now, do_div(now_ns, NSEC_PER_SEC));
+	}
+
 	/*
 	 * If the RTC alarm timer only supports a limited time offset, set the
 	 * alarm time to the maximum supported value.
-- 
